 ;; OUR export, and nobody else's. Kyutai's weights are plain CC BY 4.0, so
 ;; commercial use and redistribution of an adaptation are both permitted with
 ;; attribution - but the ready-made ONNX bundle k2-fsa publishes is a THIRD
 ;; PARTY's export, and the README inside that archive says
 ;; in as many words "It is for non-commercial" while the LICENSE it sits beside
 ;; is CC BY 4.0. We did not write either sentence and cannot resolve them, so
 ;; Vis mirrors neither: `bin/export-pocket-tts` exports Kyutai's own checkpoint
 ;; and the pack carries that. The export is proven before it is published - the
 ;; script clones a clip, speaks a sentence, and Parakeet reads it back.
 ;;
 ;; The entry stays opt-in because it is a 96 MB download for an engine that is not
 ;; the default speaker, not because of its licence: the weights are CC BY 4.0, the
 ;; export is ours, and every clip it ships was generated by Vis.
 {:id "pocket-tts-int8"
  :engine :pocket-tts
  :install-dir "sherpa-onnx-pocket-tts-int8"
  ;; The clips are REQUIRED, so an install that predates them counts as incomplete
  ;; and is replaced - a bundle with no voice cannot speak, and reporting it ready
  ;; would be a lie the first synthesis call pays for.
  :requires ["lm_flow.int8.onnx" "lm_main.int8.onnx" "encoder.onnx" "decoder.int8.onnx"
             "text_conditioner.onnx" "vocab.json" "token_scores.json"
             "voices/kristin.wav" "voices/cori.wav" "voices/john.wav"]
  ;; Pocket clones a REFERENCE CLIP instead of selecting a baked speaker, so a voice
  ;; is a WAV inside the bundle. Kyutai's own clips are not ours to hand on; these
  ;; three were generated by `bin/make-voice-clips` from PUBLIC-DOMAIN Piper voices,
  ;; which makes them Vis' to publish. `:clip-text` is what the clip says, and it is
  ;; passed to the model as the reference transcript - the clone tracks a voice far
  ;; more closely when it knows the words it is listening to.
  :voices
  [{:id "kristin"
    :label "Kristin (en-US)"
    :language "en-US"
    :gender :female
    :clip "voices/kristin.wav"
    :clip-text
    "This is a reference recording, read at an ordinary pace. Nothing in it is difficult to say, and that is the point: what should carry is the sound of the voice, not the words it happens to be reading."}
   {:id "cori"
    :label "Cori (en-GB)"
    :language "en-GB"
    :gender :female
    :clip "voices/cori.wav"
    :clip-text
    "A short paragraph, read aloud without hurry, with the pauses left where they fall. Ten seconds of plain speech is enough to hear how a voice moves from one sentence into the next."}
   {:id "john"
    :label "John (en-US)"
    :language "en-US"
    :gender :male
    :clip "voices/john.wav"
    :clip-text
    "Reading quietly for a few seconds, plainly and at a steady pace. The manner matters more than the meaning here: where the breath lands, how a sentence turns, and what an unhurried voice sounds like."}]
